QB.push(
{id:1001,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A chat backend appends the user's typed text as one message and their uploaded caption as a second message, both with `role: \"user\"`, then calls `POST /v1/messages`. No error is returned, but the model behaves as though it received one long user turn and the team's per-message logic breaks. What happened?",
o:["A. Consecutive turns with the same role are combined into a single turn before the model sees them","B. The request is rejected with `400 invalid_request_error` because roles must strictly alternate","C. The second `user` message is silently discarded and only the first is used","D. The API inserts an empty `assistant` turn between them to preserve alternation"],a:0,
e:"The Messages API documents that **consecutive `user` or `assistant` turns in your request are combined into a single turn**. Nothing errors, nothing is dropped — the two blocks of text are concatenated into one rendered user turn. Models are trained on alternating turns, so the API normalises to that shape. The transferable principle: the wire format is not a faithful record of your application's data model. If two pieces of user input must stay logically distinct, express that inside one message with separate content blocks and your own labelling, not by emitting two same-role messages.",
w:"B — alternation is not enforced; same-role runs are merged, not rejected. C — no content is discarded; both texts reach the model. D — the API never synthesises assistant turns on your behalf."},

{id:1002,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What values does the top-level `system` parameter of a Messages API request accept?",
o:["A. Only a plain string","B. Either a plain string or an array of text content blocks","C. An array of message objects, each with a `role` and `content`","D. A single object of the form `{\"type\":\"system\",\"text\":\"...\"}`"],a:1,
e:"`system` accepts **either a string or an array of text blocks**. The array form matters in practice: content blocks are where per-block metadata such as `cache_control` can be attached, so a long stable instruction set can be split into a cached prefix and a volatile tail. Note the shape difference — `system` blocks are content blocks (`{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":...}`), not messages, and they carry no `role`. The top-level `system` parameter is also the only way to set instructions that apply from the very first turn, since a `system`-role entry can never be `messages[0]`.",
w:"A — the array-of-text-blocks form is also valid and is what enables per-block `cache_control`. C — that is the shape of `messages`, not `system`; system blocks have no `role`. D — there is no `system` content-block type; the blocks inside `system` are ordinary `text` blocks."},

{id:1003,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What forms may the `content` field of an entry in the `messages` array take?",
o:["A. It must always be an array of content-block objects","B. It must always be a plain string","C. Either a plain string or an array of content-block objects","D. A string for `user` messages and an array of blocks for `assistant` messages"],a:2,
e:"`content` accepts **a plain string or an array of content blocks**. The string form is shorthand for a single `text` block — `\"hi\"` and `[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"hi\"}]` are equivalent. The block form is required as soon as a message carries anything other than plain text: images, documents, `tool_use`, `tool_result`, or `thinking`. Because responses always come back as an array, code that reads or replays assistant turns should assume the array form and iterate; the string shorthand is a request-side convenience only.",
w:"A — the string shorthand is valid and expands to a single `text` block. B — multimodal and tool content require the array form. D — the choice of form is not tied to role; either role may use either form."},

{id:1004,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team builds a user message from three content blocks — a document, a short instruction, and a reference table — and assembles the array in whatever order their template loop happens to emit. Output quality varies run to run between deployments with identical inputs. What is the mechanical explanation?",
o:["A. Content blocks are reordered server-side into a canonical order, so template order is irrelevant","B. Only the first content block in a message is read; the rest are metadata","C. Blocks of different types are dispatched to separate model passes and merged non-deterministically","D. Blocks are rendered into the prompt in array order, so a different array order is a different prompt"],a:3,
e:"A multi-block message is flattened into the prompt in **array order**. The `content` array is an ordered sequence, not an unordered bag, so shuffling blocks produces a genuinely different prompt and can shift behaviour. This is why Anthropic's guidance places long documents and images *before* the instruction text: the model reads the material first, then the ask. The transferable principle: block order is part of your prompt and belongs under version control, not left to iteration order over a dictionary or template map.",
w:"A — there is no server-side canonicalisation of block order. B — every block in the array is read; none is treated as metadata. C — a message is rendered into one prompt for one forward pass, not split per block type."},

{id:1005,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"At turn 40 of a long support conversation, an operator needs to inject a new policy instruction (\"do not discuss pricing\") for the remainder of the session. Editing the top-level `system` parameter works but destroys the cache hit rate on every subsequent request. On a model that supports it, what is the mechanically correct alternative?",
o:["A. Append a `system`-role message to the end of `messages` rather than editing top-level `system`","B. Insert the instruction as `messages[0]` with `role: \"system\"`","C. Send the instruction as a second top-level `system` parameter alongside the first","D. Wrap the instruction in a `tool_result` block so it is treated as authoritative"],a:0,
e:"Some newer models accept a **`system` role inside `messages` mid-conversation**. Appending one leaves the cacheable prefix — rendered in the order **tools → system → messages** — byte-identical, so the cached prefix survives while the new instruction still carries operator authority. Editing top-level `system` changes the prefix near its front and invalidates everything after it. Constraint to remember: a `system`-role entry may never be `messages[0]`; instructions needed from turn one belong in the top-level `system` parameter. **Currency note:** mid-conversation `system` messages are model-dependent — verify support before relying on them.",
w:"B — a `system`-role entry is explicitly not allowed as `messages[0]`; that is a validation error. C — `system` is a single top-level parameter; there is no second one. D — `tool_result` blocks are tool output, must carry a matching `tool_use_id`, and confer no authority."},

{id:1006,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent loop stores each assistant turn as the concatenated text of its `text` blocks and replays that string on the next request. It works for plain chat. As soon as tools are enabled, tool calls stop resolving and some requests return `400`. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Store the assistant turn as a `tool_result` block instead of a `text` block","B. Append the response's full `content` array back into `messages` verbatim","C. Set `tool_choice: {\"type\":\"any\"}` so the model re-issues the lost call","D. Store `stop_reason` alongside the text and replay it as part of the message"],a:1,
e:"The assistant turn is the **whole `content` array**, not its text. Flattening it discards `tool_use` blocks — including the `id` a later `tool_result` must reference — and any `thinking` blocks the model expects returned unchanged. Once the `tool_use` block is gone, a matching `tool_result` has nothing to pair with, which is a `400`. Because the API is stateless, whatever you replay *is* the conversation; there is no server-side copy to fall back on. The rule: append `response.content` unmodified, then add your new user message after it.",
w:"A — `tool_result` blocks belong in a `user` message and describe tool output, not the assistant's turn. C — forcing a tool call papers over the lost history and changes model behaviour; the pairing error remains. D — `stop_reason` is response metadata and is not part of any message you send back."},

{id:1007,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What may the `content` field of a `tool_result` block contain?",
o:["A. A plain string only","B. A plain string or a number, but never structured data","C. A plain string or an array of content blocks, which may include images","D. Only a JSON object matching the tool's `input_schema`"],a:2,
e:"`tool_result.content` accepts **a string or an array of content blocks, and those blocks may include images**. That matters for tools that return visual output — a chart renderer, a screenshot tool, a map tile fetcher — because the rendered result can be handed straight back to the model rather than described in prose. The block also carries `tool_use_id` (mandatory, must match the originating `tool_use.id`) and optionally `is_error: true`. Remember tool results always travel in a **`user`** message; there is no `tool` role in this API.",
w:"A — the array-of-blocks form is valid and is how image results are returned. B — structured data is fine; results are commonly JSON serialised to a string. D — `input_schema` constrains the tool's *input*, not the shape of its result; results are unschematised."},

{id:1008,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A refactor introduces an off-by-one bug: the new user message is inserted into `messages` one position *before* the previous assistant turn. No error is returned and requests succeed with `200`, but replies are subtly incoherent and reference things out of order. Why did nothing fail loudly?",
o:["A. The API validates ordering only when `tools` is present on the request","B. The SDK silently re-sorts `messages` by timestamp, so the fault is in the SDK","C. Ordering errors surface as `stop_reason: \"refusal\"` rather than an HTTP error","D. The API is stateless and renders `messages` in the order given; it has no notion of the \"correct\" order"],a:3,
e:"There is **no server-side conversation state to validate against**. The service holds no history of your session, so it cannot know that turn 12 was supposed to precede turn 13 — it renders the array you sent, in the order you sent it, and generates the next turn. Structural rules that *are* enforced (unmatched `tool_use` ids, a `system` entry at `messages[0]`) produce `400`; semantic ordering is entirely your responsibility. The transferable principle: statelessness moves conversation integrity into your code, and integrity bugs there fail silently as quality regressions, not as errors.",
w:"A — `tools` changes which structural rules apply, not whether ordering is checked; ordering never is. B — the SDKs transmit `messages` as given; they do not reorder. C — `refusal` is a safety outcome, unrelated to message ordering."},

{id:1009,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which content block types are valid inside a `user` message?",
o:["A. `text`, `image`, `document`, and `tool_result`","B. `text` and `image` only","C. `text`, `tool_use`, and `tool_result`","D. `text`, `image`, and `thinking`"],a:0,
e:"A `user` message may carry **`text`, `image`, `document`, and `tool_result`** blocks. The asymmetry with assistant turns is the point worth internalising: `tool_use` is something the *model* emits, so it appears in assistant content; `tool_result` is something *you* supply, so it appears in user content. `thinking` blocks are likewise model-produced — you replay them inside the assistant turn you received them in, never author them in a user message. Getting this direction backwards is a common source of `400 invalid_request_error`.",
w:"B — `document` and `tool_result` are also valid in user content. C — `tool_use` is emitted by the model in an assistant turn; sending it as user content is invalid. D — `thinking` blocks are model-produced and belong to the assistant turn, not a user message."},

{id:1010,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A code generator sets `stop_sequences: [\"</code>\"]` and splits the returned text on `</code>` to isolate the block. `stop_reason` comes back as `stop_sequence` on every call, but the split always yields exactly one element and the parser reports a missing delimiter. What is the cause?",
o:["A. `stop_sequences` matches case-insensitively, so a differently-cased tag was actually matched","B. The matched stop sequence is not included in the returned text; read the `stop_sequence` field to know which one fired","C. The delimiter is returned but moved to the end of the `usage` object rather than `content`","D. Only the first character of a multi-character stop sequence is emitted before generation halts"],a:1,
e:"Generation stops **before** the stop sequence is emitted, so the trigger text never appears in `content`. The response tells you what happened through two fields: `stop_reason: \"stop_sequence\"` and the **`stop_sequence`** field, which names the specific sequence that fired — essential when you pass several. The fix is to stop treating the sequence as an in-band delimiter and instead branch on the response metadata, appending the delimiter yourself if a downstream consumer needs it. Transferable principle: control information belongs in response fields, not scraped out of generated text.",
w:"A — stop sequences match exactly; there is no case folding. C — `usage` reports token counts only and never carries generated text. D — no part of the stop sequence is emitted; the halt precedes it entirely."},

{id:1011,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which roles may appear on entries in the `messages` array?",
o:["A. `user`, `assistant`, and `tool`","B. `user`, `assistant`, and `function`","C. `user` and `assistant`, with some newer models additionally accepting `system` mid-conversation","D. `user`, `assistant`, and `developer`"],a:2,
e:"The core roles are **`user` and `assistant`**. Some newer models additionally accept a **`system` role mid-conversation** — useful for injecting an operator instruction without disturbing the cached prefix — but never as `messages[0]`; first-turn instructions go in the top-level `system` parameter. Critically, there is **no `tool` role** in this API: tool output is returned as `tool_result` content blocks inside a **`user`** message. Developers arriving from other providers' schemas reach for a `tool` or `function` role and get a `400`.",
w:"A — there is no `tool` role; tool output is a `tool_result` block in a `user` message. B — `function` is another provider's convention, not this API's. D — there is no `developer` role in the Messages API."},

{id:1012,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team adds three well-described tools to a request and expects the model to call `lookup_order` for the question \"where is my parcel?\". Instead it replies with a plain text apology and no `tool_use` block. `tool_choice` was not set. What does this tell them?",
o:["A. Supplying `tools` without `tool_choice` disables tool use entirely for that request","B. `tool_choice` must be set to `{\"type\":\"none\"}` to enable optional tool use","C. The tools were rejected at validation and silently stripped from the request","D. `tool_choice` defaults to `{\"type\":\"auto\"}`, so tools are offered rather than mandated"],a:3,
e:"`tool_choice` defaults to **`{\"type\":\"auto\"}`**: the tools are available and the model decides. A text-only reply is therefore a legitimate outcome, not a malfunction, and the lever is prompting and tool descriptions — be prescriptive about *when* to call each tool — rather than plumbing. If a call genuinely must happen, `any` or `tool` force one, but note they are constrained: they error under manual extended thinking and return `400` on some models, whereas `auto` and `none` always work. **Currency note:** prefer `auto` plus an explicit instruction, with `strict: true` if you need schema-valid arguments.",
w:"A — omitting `tool_choice` yields `auto`, which enables tool use; it does not disable it. B — `none` forbids tool use, the opposite of what they want. C — invalid tool definitions cause a `400`; they are never silently stripped."},

{id:1013,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A research assistant uses server-side web search. Some responses come back with `stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"`. The team's handler appends a new user message reading \"Continue.\" and re-calls the API. Results are erratic and occasionally the model restarts its search from scratch. What is the correct continuation?",
o:["A. Re-send the conversation with the paused assistant `content` appended and no extra user message","B. Re-send the original user message alone so the server resumes from its own checkpoint","C. Set `tool_choice: {\"type\":\"any\"}` on the follow-up to force the search to resume","D. Treat `pause_turn` as terminal and surface the partial answer to the user"],a:0,
e:"`pause_turn` means the **server-side sampling loop hit its iteration limit** mid-task. To resume, send the conversation back with the paused **assistant `content` appended unchanged** — the trailing server-tool block is the signal to continue, and the server picks up from there. Injecting a \"Continue.\" user message changes the prompt and invites the model to reinterpret the task, which is exactly the erratic behaviour observed. Bound the loop with a continuation cap so a pathological task cannot ping-pong indefinitely.",
w:"B — there is no server-side checkpoint; dropping the assistant turn discards the work in progress. C — forcing a tool call does not resume a paused turn and distorts the model's choice. D — `pause_turn` is explicitly resumable; treating it as terminal throws away a partially completed task."},

{id:1014,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A compliance dashboard must count how often Claude declines a request. The current implementation greps response text for phrases like \"I can't help with that\" and \"I'm unable to\". Counts are wildly unreliable in both directions. What is the correct mechanism?",
o:["A. Watch for HTTP `403 permission_error`, which is how declines are surfaced","B. Branch on `stop_reason: \"refusal\"` and read `stop_details` for the category","C. Count responses whose `content` array is empty, which indicates a decline","D. Enable `output_config.format` so declines are returned as a typed JSON field"],a:1,
e:"A decline is a first-class response state: HTTP `200` with **`stop_reason: \"refusal\"`**, and a `stop_details` object naming the policy category that triggered it. `stop_details` is populated *only* for refusals and is `null` for every other stop reason, so guard before reading it. String matching fails both ways — it misses paraphrases and false-positives on a model merely discussing inability. Transferable principle: when the API exposes a typed signal for a condition, never re-derive that condition from generated prose.",
w:"A — `403 permission_error` is an authorisation failure on your credential, unrelated to content declines. C — a refusal normally still returns explanatory text; an empty `content` array is not the marker. D — structured output constrains format; it does not classify declines, and a refusal may not match your schema at all."},

{id:1015,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"When is the `stop_sequence` field on a Messages API response populated?",
o:["A. On every response, echoing the first entry of the request's `stop_sequences` array","B. Whenever `stop_reason` is either `stop_sequence` or `max_tokens`","C. Only when `stop_reason` is `stop_sequence`; it is `null` otherwise","D. Only when `stop_sequences` was omitted, to report the model's internal default"],a:2,
e:"`stop_sequence` carries the **specific sequence that fired**, and only when **`stop_reason` is `stop_sequence`**; for every other stop reason it is `null`. Two consequences for client code. First, read it rather than guessing: with several sequences configured, this field is the only way to know which one matched, and it is how you route to different handlers. Second, guard it — dereferencing it unconditionally is a null-pointer bug waiting for the first `end_turn`. The same guard discipline applies to `stop_details`, which is non-null only on `refusal`.",
w:"A — it is not an echo of your request; it is populated only on an actual match. B — a `max_tokens` stop leaves it `null`; no sequence was matched. D — there are no internal default stop sequences to report."},

{id:1016,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A long-running research agent returns a response with `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` and a partially written summary. The retry wrapper resends the identical request three times, then gives up and pages an engineer. What should it do instead?",
o:["A. Retry with a longer timeout, since this is a transient capacity condition","B. Retry with a higher `max_tokens`, which is the binding limit in this case","C. Halve `max_tokens` and retry, so generation finishes inside the window","D. Treat the response as truncated and reduce the input — the window filled during generation"],a:3,
e:"`model_context_window_exceeded` means the request **plus the output generated so far** filled the context window. It is deterministic: the same request will do it again, so retrying unchanged is pure waste. Recovery means shrinking the input side — compact or trim history, drop stale tool results, summarise earlier turns — or moving to a larger-context model. Remember the underlying rule: output, including thinking tokens, is drawn from the *same* window as the input, not from a separate budget, so a near-full prompt leaves no room to answer.",
w:"A — the condition is deterministic, not transient; timeouts are irrelevant. B — raising `max_tokens` cannot help when the window itself is exhausted; the ceiling here is the window, not the cap. C — lowering `max_tokens` produces an even shorter truncated answer and does not free input space."},

{id:1017,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent loop is written as `while response.stop_reason != \"end_turn\": execute_tools(response); response = call_api()`. It runs correctly for weeks, then a request that triggers a safety decline causes the process to spin at 100% CPU and exhaust the token budget. What is the defect?",
o:["A. The loop condition treats every non-`end_turn` stop reason as \"more tools to run\", so `refusal` never exits","B. `execute_tools` should have been called before the first API request, not after","C. `refusal` responses omit `stop_reason`, so the comparison evaluates against `null`","D. The loop needs a `time.sleep` between iterations to let rate limits recover"],a:0,
e:"The loop **inverts the test**. It should continue only on the stop reasons that genuinely mean \"act and come back\" — `tool_use`, and `pause_turn` for server tools — and exit on everything else. Written as `!= \"end_turn\"`, a `refusal` (and equally `max_tokens` or `model_context_window_exceeded`) keeps the loop alive with no tools to run and no progress. Because the versioning policy permits **new values to be added to enum-like outputs**, an allowlist plus a default branch is the only shape that stays correct as the enum grows; a denylist of one value does not.",
w:"B — call order is fine; the first response must exist before tools can be executed. C — `stop_reason` is always present on a completed non-streamed response, including refusals. D — sleeping slows the spin but never terminates it; this is not a rate-limit problem."},

{id:1018,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An agent occasionally throws a JSON parse error on `tool_use.input` for one specific tool that takes a long text argument. The failing responses all show `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. What is happening and what is the right handling?",
o:["A. The tool's `input_schema` is invalid; fix the schema and the parse error disappears","B. The tool arguments were truncated mid-JSON — discard the call, raise `max_tokens`, and retry","C. Escaped Unicode in the arguments broke the parser — strip escapes before parsing","D. Parallel tool use interleaved two argument streams — set `disable_parallel_tool_use`"],a:1,
e:"`max_tokens` cuts generation at an arbitrary point, and a `tool_use` block being emitted at that moment leaves its `input` as **incomplete JSON**. The dangerous failure mode is executing a half-parsed call, so the rule is: check `stop_reason` *before* acting on `content`. On `max_tokens` the turn is untrustworthy — do not execute, raise `max_tokens` (or shrink what the tool is asked to carry), and retry. Transferable principle: `stop_reason` is a precondition on the validity of `content`, not an afterthought logged next to it.",
w:"A — a bad `input_schema` fails at request validation with a `400`, not intermittently at parse time. C — escaping quirks are real and argue for a proper JSON parser, but they do not correlate with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. D — parallel calls arrive as separate, individually complete blocks; they are not interleaved into one stream."},

{id:1019,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What is true of the `stop_details` object on a Messages API response?",
o:["A. It is present on every response and reports the token position at which generation stopped","B. It replaces `stop_reason` on models that support it","C. It is populated only when `stop_reason` is `refusal`, and is `null` otherwise","D. It lists every stop sequence that was configured but not matched"],a:2,
e:"`stop_details` is populated **only when `stop_reason` is `refusal`**, where it identifies the policy category behind the decline; for `end_turn`, `max_tokens`, `tool_use`, `pause_turn` and the rest it is `null`. It complements rather than replaces `stop_reason`: you branch on `stop_reason`, then read `stop_details` inside the refusal branch for routing or telemetry. Treat the category set as open — new categories can appear — so log the raw value and default gracefully rather than exhaustively switching on it.",
w:"A — it is absent (null) for non-refusal stops and does not report token positions. B — `stop_reason` remains the primary discriminator on every response. D — configured-but-unmatched sequences are never reported; the matched one appears in `stop_sequence`."},

{id:1020,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A gateway service normalises Claude responses for internal consumers. Its rule is: if `stop_reason` is `end_turn`, return the text; otherwise treat the call as failed and retry it up to three times. After tools are enabled, token spend triples and some user requests never complete. What is wrong with the rule?",
o:["A. Retries must use exponential backoff; the fixed retry interval is causing the spend","B. `end_turn` is not returned when `tools` is present, so every tool-enabled call is retried","C. `stop_reason` should be read from the `usage` object once tools are enabled","D. `tool_use` and `pause_turn` are continuation states, not failures — retrying restarts work instead of advancing it"],a:3,
e:"`stop_reason` is a **control-flow discriminator, not a success flag**. `tool_use` and `pause_turn` mean \"the model is mid-task, do something and come back with the result appended\"; retrying the same request instead throws away the turn and asks for it again, so the conversation never advances and every attempt is billed. The correct shape is three branches: continue (`tool_use`, `pause_turn`), terminal-and-usable (`end_turn`, `stop_sequence`), terminal-and-degraded (`max_tokens`, `refusal`, `model_context_window_exceeded`) — plus a default branch, because the enum may grow.",
w:"A — backoff shapes retry timing but the calls should not be retried at all. B — `end_turn` is returned normally on tool-enabled requests once the model has finished. C — `usage` carries token counts only; `stop_reason` is a top-level field regardless of tools."},

{id:1021,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An evaluation harness records a call as \"successful\" whenever the HTTP status is `200`. Aggregate quality scores look healthy, yet support tickets report answers that stop mid-sentence and requests the assistant declined. What single response field would have exposed both?",
o:["A. `stop_reason`, because `200` also covers `max_tokens`, `refusal` and `model_context_window_exceeded`","B. `usage.output_tokens`, because degraded responses always report zero","C. The `request-id` header, which encodes the completion state","D. `content[0].type`, which is `error` on degraded turns"],a:0,
e:"HTTP status answers \"did the call succeed?\"; **`stop_reason` answers \"is the answer usable?\"** A truncated turn (`max_tokens`), a decline (`refusal`) and a filled context window (`model_context_window_exceeded`) are all `200` responses. Any harness or dashboard that conflates transport success with semantic success is blind to exactly the failures users notice. Emit `stop_reason` as a labelled metric dimension so a rise in truncation or refusal is visible without reading transcripts.",
w:"B — a truncated response has plenty of output tokens; a refusal usually has some too. C — `request-id` is an opaque correlation identifier and encodes nothing about the outcome. D — there is no `error` content-block type; errors are non-`200` responses with an error body."},

{id:1022,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A markdown report generator sets `stop_sequences: [\"\\n\\n\"]` to \"keep sections tidy\". Every response is a single short paragraph and `stop_reason` is always `stop_sequence`. What is the flaw?",
o:["A. `stop_sequences` entries cannot contain escape characters, so the value is being misparsed","B. The chosen sequence is ordinary output formatting, so it matches at the first blank line","C. `stop_sequences` applies to the input prompt, not the generated output","D. A single-element `stop_sequences` array is ignored; at least two entries are required"],a:1,
e:"Stop sequences are matched against the **generated text**, so any string the model would naturally produce becomes a premature halt. A blank line is the most common markdown token there is, hence a one-paragraph answer every time. Choose delimiters the model would never emit incidentally — a distinctive sentinel such as `---END---` — and remember two mechanics: generation stops *before* the sequence is emitted, and the matched value is reported in the `stop_sequence` field. Length control belongs to `max_tokens` and prompting, not to stop sequences.",
w:"A — escape sequences in JSON string values are fine; the sequence really is matching. C — stop sequences act on generated output; nothing is matched against the prompt. D — a single-entry array is valid and fully honoured."},

{id:1023,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Which `stop_reason` indicates that the model's own context window filled up while it was generating, rather than that your configured output cap was reached?",
o:["A. `max_tokens`","B. `pause_turn`","C. `model_context_window_exceeded`","D. `end_turn`"],a:2,
e:"**`model_context_window_exceeded`** means generation ran into the *window*; **`max_tokens`** means it ran into *your cap*. The distinction drives different remedies: `max_tokens` is fixed by raising your cap, while a filled window can only be fixed by reducing input — trimming history, dropping stale tool results, or moving to a larger-context model. The underlying rule is that the context window bounds the request **plus** the generated output including thinking tokens; output is not a separate budget, so raising `max_tokens` cannot buy space that does not exist.",
w:"A — `max_tokens` is your own configured ceiling, not the model's window. B — `pause_turn` is a resumable pause in the server-side tool loop. D — `end_turn` means the model finished naturally."},

{id:1024,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team adds a response cache keyed by a hash of the request body. Hit rate is good, but users start reporting that certain questions *always* return a half-finished answer, permanently. What policy is missing?",
o:["A. Key the cache on `request-id` rather than a hash of the body","B. Shorten the cache TTL so entries expire before the model changes","C. Include `max_tokens` in the cache key so different caps do not collide","D. Only cache turns whose `stop_reason` indicates a complete, usable response"],a:3,
e:"Caching is a decision about **whether a turn is worth keeping**, and `stop_reason` is the field that answers it. `max_tokens`, `refusal` and `model_context_window_exceeded` all denote degraded turns; storing one pins that degradation for every future identical request, which is precisely the \"always half-finished\" symptom. Cache on `end_turn` (and `stop_sequence` where the sequence is your intended terminator); on the degraded reasons, fix the cause and re-request. Transferable principle: never persist an output you have not validated as complete.",
w:"A — `request-id` is unique per call, so it can never produce a cache hit. B — TTL bounds staleness but a truncated entry is wrong the moment it is written. C — a sensible key hygiene point, but it does not stop truncated answers from being cached."},

{id:1025,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer sees `id: \"msg_01...\"` on each response and designs turn two to send only the new user message plus that `id`, expecting the service to look up the prior context. The model answers as if the conversation had just begun. What is the response `id` actually for?",
o:["A. It names that one message object for logging and correlation; it is not a conversation handle","B. It is a session token that must be sent in the `anthropic-session` header to resume","C. It is a cache key that restores prior context when placed in `metadata`","D. It is the identifier used to cancel an in-flight generation"],a:0,
e:"The response `id` identifies **that single message object** — useful for logging, deduplication in your own store, and correlating a stored transcript row with a response. It is not a handle to server-side state, because there is none: the Messages API is **stateless**, and the only context the model sees is the `messages` array you transmit on that call. Every architecture built on this API owns conversation persistence itself. Nothing in the request accepts a prior message `id` as a substitute for history.",
w:"B — there is no session header and no server-side session to resume. C — `metadata` carries opaque attribution fields and restores nothing. D — there is no cancel-by-message-id operation on the Messages API."},

{id:1026,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"After a quality incident, an engineer must prove which model actually served a set of requests two weeks ago. Config was changed mid-window by a deploy and the config history is incomplete. Which response field settles it, assuming it was logged?",
o:["A. `id`, whose prefix encodes the serving model","B. `model`, which the response echoes for the model that generated it","C. `usage.input_tokens`, which differs per tokenizer and so identifies the model","D. `stop_reason`, which differs by model family"],a:1,
e:"Every response echoes the **`model`** that produced it. Logging it per call gives you ground truth independent of your own configuration history, which is exactly what you need when config is the thing in doubt. This pairs with two model facts worth holding together: current IDs are **dateless pinned snapshots** — never append a date suffix — and pinning fixes the weights but not the serving infrastructure, so identical output is not guaranteed even on one ID. Log the echoed `model` alongside `stop_reason` and `usage` on every request.",
w:"A — the `msg_` prefix is generic and carries no model information. C — token counts do vary by tokenizer, but inferring a model from a count is indirect and ambiguous. D — `stop_reason` values are common across models and identify nothing about which one ran."},

{id:1027,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What does `usage.input_tokens` on a Messages API response count?",
o:["A. Only the tokens of the final user message on that request","B. Only the tokens the model actually attended to, excluding unused context","C. Every input token the request rendered on that call — system, tools, and the full message history","D. A running total of input tokens across the whole conversation, accumulated server-side"],a:2,
e:"`input_tokens` is the billed input for **that one call**: the top-level `system`, all `tools` definitions, and the entire `messages` array you transmitted. Two consequences follow. First, tool definitions are re-billed as input on every request, plus a fixed tool-use system prompt overhead — so a bloated tool surface has a per-call cost. Second, because the API is stateless and you resend history each turn, `input_tokens` grows turn over turn; there is no server-side accumulator, so the figure is per-request and yours to sum.",
w:"A — the whole rendered request is counted, not just the last message. B — the full rendered prompt is billed; there is no attention-based discount. D — nothing is accumulated server-side; each response reports only its own call."},

{id:1028,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team caps `max_tokens` at 300 to control spend on a summarisation endpoint. Roughly 30% of responses come back with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and are discarded by the caller, which then retries with a higher cap. Finance reports output spend *above* the uncapped baseline. What explains this?",
o:["A. Discarded responses are refunded only if the retry succeeds within the same minute","B. `max_tokens` raises the per-token output price when set below the model default","C. Retries bypass the cap, so the second call generates the full response at premium rates","D. The truncated output was generated and billed; the retry then pays for the full response again"],a:3,
e:"Hitting the cap does not make the tokens free. `usage.output_tokens` sits at the ceiling and you are billed for them; if the caller then discards the turn and re-requests, you pay twice for one answer. A too-tight `max_tokens` is therefore a false economy whenever truncation triggers a retry. Size the cap to the p99 useful answer length instead, and note the related trap: **`max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate limits**, so lowering it to relieve throttling achieves nothing either.",
w:"A — there is no refund mechanism for truncated generations. B — `max_tokens` is a ceiling, not a pricing modifier. C — retries are ordinary requests billed at ordinary rates; there is no premium tier involved."},

{id:1029,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An analytics job reconciles the monthly invoice by summing `usage.input_tokens + usage.output_tokens` over every logged response. On workloads that reuse a long stable prefix the sum is materially lower than the invoiced input volume. What did the job miss?",
o:["A. Cached input is reported in the separate `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `cache_read_input_tokens` fields","B. `input_tokens` is rounded down to the nearest thousand in the response","C. Tool definitions are billed but never appear in any `usage` field","D. `usage` omits the system prompt, which must be counted separately"],a:0,
e:"`usage` splits input reporting: ordinary input lands in `input_tokens`, while cached input is reported in **`cache_creation_input_tokens`** (written) and **`cache_read_input_tokens`** (served from cache). Any reconciliation that sums only the first two fields understates cached traffic. It is also the diagnostic you want for cache health — persistent zeros in `cache_read_input_tokens` across repeated requests mean something is invalidating the prefix. Cost multipliers differ too: writes bill above base input, reads far below it.",
w:"B — token counts are reported exactly, with no rounding. C — tool definitions are billed and are included in the counted input, not omitted from `usage`. D — the system prompt is part of the rendered input and is counted."},

{id:1030,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost dashboard records `usage` from the *final* response of each agent run. Engineers notice reported spend is a small fraction of the invoice for tool-heavy sessions. What is the correct accounting unit?",
o:["A. The final response's `usage`, multiplied by the number of tools defined","B. The sum of `usage` across every API call in the loop, since each iteration resends the whole history","C. The first response's `usage`, which reports the full projected turn cost","D. `usage` from the `count_tokens` call made before the loop starts"],a:1,
e:"A tool-using turn is **many requests**, not one. Each iteration resends the accumulated history — original prompt, every prior `tool_use` block, every `tool_result` — so input tokens are re-billed and grow with each round trip, and the last response's `usage` reflects only the last call. Cost per completed task is therefore the sum over the loop. This is also the strongest argument for keeping tool results compact: an oversized result is not paid for once, it is paid for on every subsequent iteration of that turn.",
w:"A — tool count affects the per-call input size, not a multiplier on the final usage. C — the first call is the *cheapest*; history has not yet accumulated. D — `count_tokens` estimates input for one request and knows nothing about how many iterations will run."},

{id:1031,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"For a given `anthropic-version`, what does Anthropic's versioning policy guarantee, and what does it explicitly reserve the right to change?",
o:["A. Nothing is guaranteed; any parameter may be removed at any time within a version","B. Both inputs and outputs are frozen, so no new fields or enum values can ever appear","C. Existing input and output parameters are preserved, but optional inputs, new output values, and new enum-like variants may be added","D. Only inputs are preserved; output shapes may change arbitrarily"],a:2,
e:"A version **preserves existing input and output parameters**. Anthropic may still add optional inputs, add values to the output, change the conditions for specific error types, and **add new variants to enum-like output values**. That last clause is the one with teeth: it is why client code must carry a default branch for `stop_reason`, streaming event types and similar enums, and why an exhaustive switch that throws on the unknown is a latent outage rather than defensive programming. Pinning a version buys schema stability, not a closed world.",
w:"A — existing parameters are explicitly preserved within a version. B — additive changes to outputs and enums are expressly permitted. D — output parameters are preserved as well as inputs; the additions are additive, not arbitrary."},

{id:1032,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team migrates a working cURL script from an API key to a short-lived OAuth access token by pasting the token into the existing `x-api-key` header. Every request now returns `401 authentication_error`. What is the correct change?",
o:["A. Keep `x-api-key` but prefix the value with `Bearer `","B. Send the token in both `x-api-key` and `Authorization` so whichever is read succeeds","C. OAuth tokens are unsupported on raw HTTP; only the SDKs can use them","D. Send the token as `Authorization: Bearer <token>` — `x-api-key` carries an API key"],a:3,
e:"The API accepts **two credential headers**: legacy **`x-api-key`** for API keys, and **`Authorization: Bearer <token>`** for bearer tokens. They are not interchangeable value slots — the header selects how the credential is interpreted, so a bearer token in `x-api-key` fails authentication. Migrating from a key to a token is therefore a header change, not a value swap. Regardless of which you use, `anthropic-version` and `content-type: application/json` remain required on the request.",
w:"A — the `Bearer ` scheme prefix belongs to the `Authorization` header; `x-api-key` takes a bare key. B — sending contradictory credentials is not a supported fallback pattern and should not be relied on. C — raw HTTP supports bearer tokens; the SDKs simply set the header for you."},

{id:1033,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A service still sends an `anthropic-beta` header for a feature that has since become generally available. Requests succeed today. During a dependency review, what is the right call?",
o:["A. Remove the header — the feature no longer needs it, and a stale beta name is a latent `400`","B. Keep it permanently; the header is what selects the GA implementation","C. Keep it but append `-ga` to the feature name to signal the transition","D. Replace it with `anthropic-version` set to the beta's release date"],a:0,
e:"Once a feature graduates, the beta name is accepted only for a transition period and then stops being recognised — and an **unrecognised `anthropic-beta` value returns `400 invalid_request_error`**, not a warning. So the header is dead weight today and a scheduled outage tomorrow. Remove it as part of the GA migration, and note that beta headers must always be the **exact documented name**; there is no versioning or suffixing convention you can invent. The general point: opt-in flags need an owner and an exit date, or they rot in place.",
w:"B — GA behaviour is the default; the header selects nothing once the feature has graduated. C — inventing a suffix produces an unrecognised value and a `400`. D — `anthropic-version` pins the API schema and is unrelated to individual beta features."},

{id:1034,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An internal HTTP wrapper defaults to `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` and serialises the Messages request body accordingly. Every call returns `400 invalid_request_error` complaining it cannot read the body. What is required?",
o:["A. Base64-encode the body and set `content-transfer-encoding`","B. Send `content-type: application/json` with a JSON-serialised body","C. Send `content-type: text/plain`; the API sniffs the body format","D. Add `anthropic-content-format: json` alongside the form encoding"],a:1,
e:"The Messages API takes a **JSON body** and requires **`content-type: application/json`**. Together with `anthropic-version` and a credential header, that is the required header set on every call. Form encoding cannot express the request at all — `messages` is an array of objects containing nested content-block arrays — so the failure is structural rather than a mere header mismatch. This class of bug is one reason Anthropic recommends the official SDKs: they set the headers and serialise the body correctly by construction.",
w:"A — the body is plain JSON; there is no transfer-encoding step. C — there is no content sniffing; the declared content type governs parsing. D — no such header exists, and the encoding itself is the problem."},

{id:1035,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How do you enable three beta features on a single raw-HTTP Messages request?",
o:["A. Send three separate `anthropic-beta` headers, one per feature","B. Send `anthropic-beta` once with the highest-priority feature; the rest are inferred","C. Send one `anthropic-beta` header whose value is the feature names separated by commas","D. Send the feature names as a `betas` array in the request body"],a:2,
e:"Multiple betas travel as a **single `anthropic-beta` header with comma-separated feature names** — `anthropic-beta: feature1,feature2,feature3`. Use the exact documented name for each; an unrecognised name, or one your organisation lacks access to, returns `400 invalid_request_error`. The SDKs expose this as a `betas` parameter and emit the header for you, which is why body-level `betas` looks familiar — it is an SDK convenience, not the wire format. Some beta APIs additionally require an endpoint-scoped beta header on every request to that endpoint.",
w:"A — repeated flags and headers are not the documented mechanism; comma-join into one header. B — nothing is inferred; every feature you want must be named. D — `betas` is an SDK parameter; on raw HTTP the names go in the header."},

{id:1036,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A developer copies a beta feature name from a blog post into `anthropic-beta` and gets a `400` whose message names the header value as unexpected. Their teammate insists the account simply lacks entitlement and opens a billing ticket. Who is right, and how would you tell?",
o:["A. The teammate — entitlement problems always surface as `402 billing_error`","B. Neither — an unrecognised beta value is silently ignored, so the `400` is unrelated","C. The developer's diagnosis is unfalsifiable; only Anthropic support can distinguish the two","D. Both causes produce the same `400 invalid_request_error`; verify the exact name against the docs first"],a:3,
e:"An **invalid beta name and a beta your organisation cannot access both return `400 invalid_request_error`**, with a message naming the unexpected `anthropic-beta` value. The status code alone does not discriminate, so the cheap test comes first: compare the string character-for-character against the current documentation, since names follow a dated `feature-name-YYYY-MM-DD` pattern and blog posts age. Only after the name is confirmed exact does entitlement become the live hypothesis. General rule: exhaust the client-side explanation before escalating.",
w:"A — entitlement for a beta surfaces as `400`, not `402`; `402 billing_error` concerns account funding. B — unrecognised beta values are rejected, not ignored. C — it is entirely falsifiable by checking the documented name."},

{id:1037,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A service that has always used the Python SDK gains a lightweight Go sidecar that calls `POST /v1/messages` with hand-rolled HTTP. The sidecar `400`s on every request with identical credentials and body to the working Python path. What is the most likely omission?",
o:["A. `anthropic-version`, which the SDK sets automatically and hand-rolled clients must send themselves","B. A `User-Agent` header, which the API requires for client identification","C. `Accept: application/json`, without which the API refuses to serialise a response","D. `Content-Length`, which must be set explicitly for the API to read the body"],a:0,
e:"**`anthropic-version` is required on every request**, and the official SDKs set it for you — so it is invisible until someone writes raw HTTP. The required set is `anthropic-version`, `content-type: application/json`, and a credential header (`x-api-key` or `Authorization: Bearer`). This is the general hazard of migrating between an SDK path and a hand-rolled one: the SDK's implicit behaviour is not part of your request body, so a byte-identical body is no guarantee of a byte-identical request.",
w:"B — no `User-Agent` requirement is imposed. C — JSON responses are returned without an `Accept` header. D — `Content-Length` is handled by any standard HTTP client and is not an API-specific requirement."},

{id:1038,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team logs the `request-id` only on non-`200` responses. A user reports a specific answer that was coherent but factually wrong; the team wants Anthropic to trace that exact call and cannot identify it. What should the logging policy be?",
o:["A. Reconstruct the identifier by hashing the request body, which is how `request-id` is derived","B. Capture `request-id` on every response, successful ones included","C. Enable `metadata.user_id`, which Anthropic can use in place of `request-id`","D. Rely on the response `id`, which is the identifier Anthropic support resolves"],a:1,
e:"**`request-id`** is the per-call correlation identifier — a response header, and also a top-level `request_id` field inside error bodies. Logging it only on failures assumes the only interesting calls are the ones that errored, but the calls that matter most in production are frequently the ones that returned `200` and were wrong. Capture it unconditionally, alongside `id`, `model`, `stop_reason` and `usage`, and index your transcript store by it. Note the spelling: it is `request-id`, not `x-request-id`.",
w:"A — it is server-assigned and opaque; it cannot be recomputed from the body. C — `metadata.user_id` is your own attribution label, not a per-call trace handle. D — the message `id` identifies the message object; `request-id` is the identifier for the API call itself."},

{id:1039,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"On a successful non-streamed Messages response, what are the values of the top-level `type` and `role` fields?",
o:["A. `type` mirrors the first content block's type; `role` mirrors the last message's role","B. `type` is `\"completion\"`; `role` is absent on responses","C. `type` is always `\"message\"` and `role` is always `\"assistant\"`","D. `type` is `\"message\"`; `role` is `\"user\"` because the response answers the user"],a:2,
e:"A Messages response is a message object: **`type: \"message\"`** and **`role: \"assistant\"`**, invariably. They look redundant but they are load-bearing for replay — the object you receive is shape-compatible with an entry in the `messages` array you send back, so appending `{role, content}` from the response is exactly how you carry a conversation forward under a stateless API. On streamed responses these fields arrive in `message_start`, whose skeleton message has empty `content` and a null `stop_reason`.",
w:"A — neither field is derived from content or from your request. B — `\"completion\"` belongs to the retired legacy Text Completions API. D — the response is the assistant's turn; `role` is never `\"user\"`."},

{id:1040,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A cost model estimates output spend from the visible character count of each answer. On reasoning-heavy prompts the invoice is several times the estimate, though `usage.output_tokens` in the logs matches the invoice exactly. What does the model fail to account for?",
o:["A. Output tokens are billed twice on reasoning-heavy prompts, once for planning and once for text","B. `output_tokens` includes the resent input history on multi-turn requests","C. Character-to-token ratio is fixed, so the estimate cannot be wrong; the logs must be corrupt","D. `output_tokens` counts thinking tokens as well as the visible text"],a:3,
e:"Generated reasoning is **output**: thinking tokens are counted in `usage.output_tokens`, billed at output rates, and drawn against both `max_tokens` and the context window. A short visible answer can therefore sit on top of a large generation. Estimating from visible characters systematically under-reads exactly the workloads where reasoning does the work. Use the reported `usage` as the source of truth and steer depth with **`output_config.effort`**. **Currency note:** fixed thinking budgets via `thinking.budget_tokens` are removed on current models and return `400`.",
w:"A — there is no double-billing; there is simply more generated output than is displayed. B — input history is reported in `input_tokens`; `output_tokens` covers only generation. C — the logs agreeing with the invoice is evidence the logs are right and the estimator is wrong."},

{id:1041,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A document Q&A feature enables citations on its `document` blocks. Answers immediately appear truncated in the UI — often just the opening clause — although `stop_reason` is `end_turn` and `usage.output_tokens` looks normal. The UI renders `content[0].text`. What changed?",
o:["A. With citations enabled the response splits into several `text` blocks; every block must be concatenated in order","B. Citations move the answer into a `citations` block, so `text` blocks now hold only a preamble","C. Citations force `stop_reason` to remain `end_turn` even on truncation, masking a real cut-off","D. Citations require streaming, and non-streamed responses return only the first block"],a:0,
e:"Enabling citations changes the **shape** of the response, not its length: the answer is split across **multiple `text` blocks**, and the cited ones carry a `citations` array with `cited_text`, `document_index` and a location (`char_location` for text, `page_location` for PDFs). A renderer that reads `content[0].text` therefore shows one fragment. The rule that generalises: `content` is an ordered list whose length and block mix depend on the features you enabled — always iterate and dispatch on `type`.",
w:"B — the citation metadata rides on the `text` blocks; there is no separate block type holding the answer. C — `stop_reason` reporting is unaffected by citations. D — citations work on non-streamed responses; nothing is withheld."},

{id:1042,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"In what order do blocks appear in a response's `content` array?",
o:["A. Grouped by type, with all `text` blocks first and all `tool_use` blocks last","B. In generation order, so a `thinking` block appears before the text and tool calls that followed it","C. In reverse generation order, newest first","D. In an unspecified order; clients must sort by an `index` field before use"],a:1,
e:"Blocks appear in **generation order** — the sequence in which the model produced them. That is why replaying `response.content` verbatim preserves the model's own reasoning and action sequence, and why a client must dispatch on each block's `type` rather than assume a position. There is no grouping and no sorting step for non-streamed responses. Streaming exposes the same ordering incrementally: each block event carries an **`index`**, and you accumulate deltas per index to rebuild the identical array.",
w:"A — blocks are not grouped by type; text and tool calls interleave as generated. C — the array is forward-ordered. D — the `index` field belongs to streaming events; a non-streamed `content` array is already ordered."},

{id:1043,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What must the `tool_use_id` field on a `tool_result` block be set to?",
o:["A. The name of the tool that was invoked","B. The `id` of the response message that contained the tool call","C. The `id` of the specific `tool_use` block the result answers","D. A client-generated UUID, which the API records for correlation"],a:2,
e:"`tool_use_id` must equal the **`id` of the specific `tool_use` block** being answered. That id is how results are paired with calls, which is what makes parallel tool use work: several `tool_use` blocks in one assistant turn are matched to several `tool_result` blocks by id, independent of position. Two failure modes follow directly — a `tool_use` left without a matching result, and a `tool_result` naming an id that does not exist, both `400 invalid_request_error`. The id is server-generated; never invent one.",
w:"A — tool names are not unique per call, so they cannot identify which invocation is being answered. B — the message `id` identifies the whole turn, not the individual call. D — ids come from the `tool_use` blocks the model emitted; client-generated values will not match."},

{id:1044,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent receives an assistant turn with three `tool_use` blocks. To keep its code simple it executes them one at a time and sends each `tool_result` in its own separate `user` message across three requests. Nothing errors, but over the following weeks the model increasingly issues one tool call per turn instead of batching. What is the cause?",
o:["A. The model treats separated results as errors and reduces its tool usage defensively","B. Sequential requests exceed the tool-use system prompt budget, disabling parallel calls","C. Each additional request invalidates the tool definitions, so only one tool remains visible","D. Splitting results across messages models a one-call-at-a-time pattern, which the model then imitates"],a:3,
e:"All `tool_result` blocks for one assistant turn belong in a **single `user` message**. The transcript you resend is the model's only evidence of how this conversation works; a history in which every assistant turn is followed by exactly one result **demonstrates** serial tool use, and the model follows the demonstrated pattern. The cost is latency and round trips. Execute the calls concurrently, then return every result together — including failures, as `tool_result` with `is_error: true` — in one user message.",
w:"A — nothing is flagged as an error; the behaviour shift is imitation of the transcript. B — there is no per-request budget that toggles parallel calls off. C — tool definitions are resent on every request and stay fully visible."},

{id:1045,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A booking agent calls `check_availability`, which throws a timeout. The developer catches the exception, logs it, and sends the next request with the `tool_result` block omitted and a `user` text message reading \"that tool failed, try something else\". The request returns `400`. What should have been sent?",
o:["A. A `tool_result` block with the matching `tool_use_id`, the error text as `content`, and `is_error: true`","B. The same assistant turn again, with the `tool_use` block stripped out","C. A `tool_result` with an empty `content` string and no `tool_use_id`","D. A `system`-role message describing the failure so the model treats it as authoritative"],a:0,
e:"Every `tool_use` block must be answered by a `tool_result` carrying its `tool_use_id`; leaving one unanswered is `400 invalid_request_error`, which is why the text-message workaround failed. A failure is still an answer: set **`is_error: true`** and put an informative message in `content`. The model then typically acknowledges the failure and either retries differently or asks for clarification — behaviour you lose entirely if you swallow the error. Transferable principle: surface tool failures to the model in-band and in the format it expects, rather than narrating them out of band.",
w:"B — editing the assistant turn to remove the call rewrites history and discards the model's decision. C — a `tool_result` without `tool_use_id` cannot be paired and is invalid. D — a system message is not a tool result; the unanswered `tool_use` still produces a `400`."},

{id:1046,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An agent runs three tool calls concurrently and appends each `tool_result` to the outgoing `user` message as it completes, so the results are in completion order rather than the order of the original `tool_use` blocks. A reviewer flags it as a bug. Are they right?",
o:["A. Yes — results must appear in the same order as the `tool_use` blocks or the API returns `400`","B. No — pairing is by `tool_use_id`, so order within the message does not affect matching","C. Yes — mismatched order silently assigns each result to the wrong call","D. No — but only because parallel tool use is disabled by default, so there is one result anyway"],a:1,
e:"Results are matched to calls by **`tool_use_id`**, not by position, so completion-order appends are correct and safe. What *is* required: every `tool_use` gets exactly one `tool_result`, all of them in one `user` message. Keep the distinction clear — positional coupling is a real hazard elsewhere in this API (the `content` array is order-sensitive; batch results are not order-guaranteed and must be keyed by `custom_id`), but tool pairing is explicitly id-based, which is what makes concurrent execution straightforward.",
w:"A — no ordering constraint is imposed on `tool_result` blocks. C — id-based matching cannot misassign results; misassignment requires a wrong id. D — parallel tool use is enabled by default; `disable_parallel_tool_use` is the opt-out."},

{id:1047,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What happens if a request contains a `tool_result` block whose `tool_use_id` matches no `tool_use` block anywhere in the conversation?",
o:["A. The block is ignored and generation proceeds normally","B. The model receives it as an unsolicited observation and usually incorporates it","C. The request is rejected with `400 invalid_request_error`","D. It is accepted, and the API assigns it to the most recent unanswered `tool_use`"],a:2,
e:"Tool pairing is validated structurally: an id that matches nothing is **`400 invalid_request_error`**, as is a `tool_use` block left without a matching result. This strictness is a feature — it makes broken history-management fail loudly at the boundary rather than silently corrupting the transcript. Practically, it is the error you see when code flattens assistant turns to plain text, drops the assistant turn entirely, or replays a stale result after a conversation was edited or branched.",
w:"A — unmatched ids are rejected, not ignored. B — there is no unsolicited-observation path; `tool_result` is strictly a reply to a specific call. D — the API performs no heuristic assignment; ids must match exactly."},

{id:1048,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"An operations agent exposes `create_ticket` and `close_ticket`. The model sometimes emits both in one assistant turn, and running them concurrently causes a race that closes tickets before they exist. The team tries prompting the model to \"call one tool at a time\", which mostly works but not always. What is the reliable mechanism?",
o:["A. Set `tool_choice: {\"type\":\"tool\", \"name\": ...}` on every request to pin a single tool","B. Split the tools across two requests so only one is ever available","C. Return the second `tool_result` with `is_error: true` to teach the model to serialise","D. Set `disable_parallel_tool_use: true` so at most one tool call is issued per response"],a:3,
e:"Parallel tool use is **on by default**; **`disable_parallel_tool_use: true`** (supplied alongside `tool_choice`) constrains the model to at most one call per response. Prompting is advisory and will fail under load — this is the general lesson that the model is not an enforcement mechanism. Note also that concurrency is not the only defence: your executor can serialise, and a `create` followed by a `close` on the same entity is a dependency your tool layer should validate rather than assume the model will order correctly.",
w:"A — forcing a specific tool every turn distorts the model's choice and prevents the loop from ever ending. B — a two-request split makes the second tool invisible when it is legitimately needed. C — a fabricated error trains nothing durable and misreports the outcome to the model."},

{id:1049,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A tool dispatcher routes on the serialised `tool_use.input` string with checks like `if '\"mode\": \"refund\"' in raw_input`. It passes tests but misroutes in production on inputs containing accented characters or URLs. What is the defect?",
o:["A. `tool_use.input` is delivered as JSON whose escaping may vary; parse it rather than string-matching the serialisation","B. `tool_use.input` is base64-encoded, so substring checks fail on non-ASCII payloads","C. Inputs above a size threshold are truncated in `input`, so later keys are missing","D. `input` keys are alphabetically reordered per request, so substring order is unstable"],a:0,
e:"`tool_use.input` is a **JSON object**; the exact serialisation is not part of the contract, and current models may emit different string escaping — Unicode escapes, escaped forward slashes — for semantically identical values. Any logic that matches on the serialised bytes is therefore fragile by construction. Parse with a real JSON parser (`json.loads` / `JSON.parse`) and branch on the decoded values. Pair this with **`strict: true`** on the tool definition when you need a hard guarantee that `input` validates against your schema.",
w:"B — `input` is plain JSON, not base64. C — inputs are not silently truncated; truncation shows up as `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and invalid JSON. D — key order in JSON is not something to depend on either way, but no per-request reordering is applied."},

{id:1050,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A platform team consolidates 60 internal tools into one Claude gateway. Per-call cost rises sharply even on simple requests where the model calls nothing, and accuracy on tool selection drops. What is the primary mechanical cause of the cost rise?",
o:["A. Unused tool definitions are billed at output rates once the model reads them","B. Every tool definition is billed as input tokens on every request, plus a fixed tool-use system prompt overhead","C. Each tool definition opens a separate cache namespace, multiplying cache-write cost","D. Tool definitions are billed per tool per token generated, so cost scales with response length"],a:1,
e:"The `tools` array is part of the rendered prompt: **all definitions are billed as input tokens on every request**, whether or not any tool is called, plus a fixed system-prompt overhead for tool use. Sixty schemas is a standing per-call tax and a selection problem at once. Anthropic's guidance is to improve descriptions and consolidate overlapping tools before adding more. Where a large library is genuinely needed, **`defer_loading`** plus the tool search tool keeps deferred tools out of context entirely — though you still transmit every definition in `tools` on each request, and at least one tool must have `defer_loading: false` or the request `400`s.",
w:"A — definitions are input, billed at input rates. C — caching is prefix-based, not per-tool; tools sit at the front of the cacheable prefix. D — definition cost is per request, independent of response length."},

{id:1051,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"How many `tool_use` blocks may a single assistant message contain?",
o:["A. Exactly one; additional calls require additional turns","B. At most two, one primary and one fallback","C. Any number — a turn may request several tool calls at once","D. Any number, but only when `tool_choice` is set to `any`"],a:2,
e:"A single assistant turn may carry **any number of `tool_use` blocks**; parallel tool use is the default behaviour. Your obligations follow from that: execute them (concurrently if safe), then return **all** the corresponding `tool_result` blocks in **one `user` message**, matched by `tool_use_id`. If your side effects cannot tolerate concurrency, opt out with `disable_parallel_tool_use: true` rather than assuming one call per turn — code written on that assumption fails intermittently, only on the requests where the model batches.",
w:"A — that is the behaviour you get only by explicitly disabling parallel tool use. B — there is no two-call limit or primary/fallback concept. D — parallel calls occur under the default `auto`; `any` forces at least one call, it does not enable batching."},

{id:1052,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A router flips `tool_choice` between `{\"type\":\"auto\"}` and `{\"type\":\"none\"}` from turn to turn depending on the request class. Cache metrics show `cache_read_input_tokens` healthy for the system prompt but near zero for the conversation history. What is the explanation?",
o:["A. `tool_choice` is not part of the cacheable prefix, so this must be a separate invalidation","B. Cached message blocks expire after one turn by design; only system blocks persist","C. `tool_choice: none` disables prompt caching entirely for that request","D. Any change to `tool_choice` invalidates cached message blocks, while tools and system blocks stay cached"],a:3,
e:"Changing `tool_choice` **invalidates cached `messages` blocks while leaving the tools and system portions cached** — which is exactly the split the metrics show. The cacheable prefix is rendered **tools → system → messages**, so per-request variation should be pushed as late as possible; flipping a parameter that affects the message rendering on alternate turns guarantees a miss on the largest and fastest-growing part of the prefix. Route by request class *before* choosing a configuration, and keep one configuration stable per conversation.",
w:"A — the invalidation is documented and specific to message blocks; nothing else need be involved. B — cached blocks live for the TTL and are refreshed on each read; they do not expire per turn. C — caching remains active; `none` invalidates message blocks like any other change."},

{id:1053,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team declares Anthropic's server-side web search tool and writes a client-side handler for it, mirroring their custom-tool code. The handler never fires, and search results appear in responses anyway. What did they misunderstand?",
o:["A. Server-side tools execute on Anthropic's infrastructure; their results arrive as content blocks in the same response","B. Server-side tools emit `tool_use` blocks only when `tool_choice` is `any`","C. Server-side tool results are delivered on a separate webhook, not in the response","D. Server-side tools require the handler to be registered through the Files API first"],a:0,
e:"Server-side tools run **on Anthropic's infrastructure**: you declare them in `tools` and the results come back as content blocks (`server_tool_use` plus a tool-specific `..._tool_result`) within the same response. There is no client-side execution step and no `tool_use` block for you to answer. The one control-flow hook that does exist is **`pause_turn`**, returned when the server-side sampling loop hits its iteration limit — resume by resending the conversation with the paused assistant content appended. Note too that server-tool failures arrive as `200` responses with an error object inside the result block, not as raised exceptions.",
w:"B — `tool_choice` does not convert a server-side tool into a client-executed one. C — results are inline in the response; no webhook is involved. D — the Files API stores documents and images; it plays no part in tool registration."},

{id:1054,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"To guarantee a lookup happens, an agent sets `tool_choice: {\"type\":\"tool\", \"name\":\"search_kb\"}` on every iteration of its loop. The first call works; the loop then never terminates, calling `search_kb` again after each result until the iteration cap trips. What is the fix?",
o:["A. Add `disable_parallel_tool_use: true` alongside the forced choice","B. Force the tool on the first request only, then continue the loop with `auto`","C. Keep forcing the tool but lower `max_tokens` so the loop exits on truncation","D. Return the tool result with `is_error: true` on the second call to break the cycle"],a:1,
e:"Forcing `tool_choice` means **the model must call a tool on every response it is forced on** — so it can never produce the `end_turn` your loop is waiting for. Force at most the turn where the call is genuinely mandatory, then switch to `auto`. Better still, treat forcing as a last resort: it errors under manual extended thinking and returns `400` on some models, whereas `auto` and `none` always work. **Currency note:** `auto` plus an explicit prompt instruction, with **`strict: true`** for schema-valid arguments — or `output_config.format` when the forced call only existed to extract JSON — is the current recommendation.",
w:"A — that limits calls to one *per response*; it does not let the loop finish. C — truncating output to escape a loop corrupts results and masks the real defect. D — a fabricated error misinforms the model and does not remove the forced-call constraint."},

{id:1055,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"Does lowering `max_tokens` help a service that is being throttled on its output-tokens-per-minute rate limit?",
o:["A. Yes — the limiter reserves `max_tokens` per in-flight request, so a lower cap frees capacity","B. Yes, but only on streamed requests, where the cap is checked up front","C. No — `max_tokens` does not factor into output-token rate limits","D. No, because output tokens are not a rate-limited dimension at all"],a:2,
e:"Rate limits apply on three dimensions — requests per minute, **input** tokens per minute and **output** tokens per minute — and **`max_tokens` does not factor into the output-token limit**. Nothing is reserved against the cap, so lowering it relieves no throttling; it only truncates answers, which typically provokes retries and *raises* consumption. Real remedies are concurrency control informed by the `anthropic-ratelimit-*` headers, honouring `retry-after` on `429`, moving eligible work to the Batch API, or requesting a limit increase.",
w:"A — no reservation against `max_tokens` occurs. B — streaming does not change how the cap interacts with rate limits. D — output tokens per minute is explicitly one of the limited dimensions."},

{id:1056,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A report generator sets `max_tokens: 100000` on a non-streamed request. The API is clearly still working — partial billing appears in usage reports — but the client raises a timeout before any response is returned. What is the appropriate change?",
o:["A. Raise the client timeout and add retries; the request will eventually return within the default window","B. Split the request into ten sequential calls of 10,000 tokens each and concatenate","C. Lower `max_tokens` until the response fits inside the default client timeout","D. Stream the request and assemble the final message from the stream"],a:3,
e:"A very large `max_tokens` means a long single generation, and the SDK defaults are not built for a ten-minute silent wait — which is why the SDKs effectively require **streaming** for large output ceilings. Streaming delivers incremental events, so the connection stays active and no single read blocks for the whole generation; use the SDK helper (`get_final_message()` / `finalMessage()`) if you only want the assembled result. Remember the final `stop_reason` and cumulative output usage arrive in **`message_delta`**, not `message_stop`.",
w:"A — blind retries on a request that legitimately takes minutes multiply cost and still time out. B — arbitrary splitting fragments the model's reasoning and needs stitching logic that streaming makes unnecessary. C — capping below the needed length truncates the report, which is the outcome being avoided."},

{id:1057,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A reviewer objects to a pull request that sets `max_tokens` to the model's full output ceiling on every request, arguing \"we'll be billed for the reserved tokens\". Which correction and which genuine risk should be raised?",
o:["A. Billing follows tokens actually generated, not the cap; the real risks are client timeouts on non-streamed calls and a weaker truncation signal","B. The reviewer is right — the cap is pre-charged, so it should track expected length closely","C. The reviewer is right about billing, and the cap also consumes context window space up front","D. Neither matters; `max_tokens` is advisory and the model stops when it is finished"],a:0,
e:"`max_tokens` is a **ceiling, not a reservation**: you pay for what is generated. The costs of an over-large cap are operational — a non-streamed request can outlive the client timeout, and a cap far above any plausible answer means `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` stops functioning as an early warning that a prompt is running away. Two related facts to keep straight: a cap **above the model's output ceiling returns `400`** with no silent clamping, and the cap does not interact with output-token rate limits.",
w:"B — nothing is pre-charged against the cap. C — the cap reserves no context space; the window is consumed by actual tokens as they are generated. D — `max_tokens` is enforced, not advisory; hitting it truncates the response."},

{id:1058,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A classification endpoint uses `max_tokens: 256` because the answer is a single label. On a reasoning-capable model, many responses come back with `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` and an empty or near-empty `text` block. Why?",
o:["A. Short caps are rejected below a documented minimum, and the empty text is the resulting error state","B. Generated reasoning counts against `max_tokens`, so the budget can be consumed before any visible answer","C. `max_tokens` bounds the input, so a long prompt leaves no room for the label","D. Classification requires `output_config.format`, without which the label is discarded"],a:1,
e:"Thinking tokens are generated output: they count against **`max_tokens`** and are billed as output. A cap sized only for the visible answer can therefore be exhausted before the answer is written, yielding a truncated turn with little or no text. Fix it by budgeting for reasoning plus answer, and by steering depth with **`output_config.effort`** — `low` is the right setting for routine classification. **Currency note:** fixed thinking budgets (`thinking.budget_tokens`) are removed on current models and return `400`; adaptive thinking plus `effort` is the mechanism.",
w:"A — a small `max_tokens` is valid; nothing is rejected here. C — `max_tokens` bounds output only; input is bounded by the context window. D — structured output constrains format and would not rescue a budget exhausted before generation of the answer."},

{id:1059,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Is `max_tokens` required on `POST /v1/messages`, and is it required on `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`?",
o:["A. Required on both endpoints","B. Optional on both; it defaults to the model's output ceiling","C. Required on `/v1/messages`; not required on `/v1/messages/count_tokens`","D. Optional on `/v1/messages`; required on `/v1/messages/count_tokens`"],a:2,
e:"`/v1/messages` requires **`model`, `max_tokens` and `messages`** — there is no default output cap, so omitting `max_tokens` is a `400`. The token counting endpoint measures *input* only and therefore needs no output cap: it takes `model` and `messages`, optionally `system` and `tools`, and returns a single field, **`input_tokens`**. That asymmetry is a useful reminder of what each endpoint is for — one generates and is bounded on output, the other measures and is bounded on nothing.",
w:"A — `count_tokens` has no use for an output cap and does not require one. B — `/v1/messages` has no implicit `max_tokens` default. D — the requirement runs the other way round."},

{id:1060,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A multi-tenant service lets each tenant configure `max_tokens`. After a tenant sets a very large value, all their requests fail with `400 invalid_request_error`, and a second tenant's identical value works fine because they are routed to a different model. What should the service do?",
o:["A. Catch the `400` and retry with half the value, halving until a request succeeds","B. Cap all tenants at the smallest ceiling across the supported model fleet","C. Set `max_tokens` to zero and let the model choose its own length","D. Validate the tenant's value against the target model's documented output ceiling, discoverable per model via the Models API"],a:3,
e:"Output ceilings are **per model**, and a value above the ceiling is rejected with **`400` — there is no silent clamping**. So a single global constant is wrong the moment your fleet is heterogeneous. Validate against the ceiling for the model that will actually serve the request; the Models API exposes per-model limits (`max_input_tokens` for the context window, `max_tokens` for the output cap) so the check can be data-driven rather than a hard-coded table that drifts. Reject invalid configuration at write time, with a clear message, rather than at request time.",
w:"A — a retry ladder burns latency on a deterministic, fully predictable validation error. B — flooring everyone to the weakest model needlessly limits tenants on larger models. C — `max_tokens` is a required, enforced parameter, not a hint the model may override."},

{id:1061,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A long-form writing feature regularly returns `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`. An engineer proposes the classic continuation trick: resend the conversation with the partial answer as a trailing `assistant` message so the model picks up where it stopped. On the current model this now returns `400`. What is the correct approach?",
o:["A. Raise `max_tokens` (streaming if it is large), or split the work into explicitly bounded sections","B. Send the partial text as a `tool_result` block so the model treats it as its own prior output","C. Send the partial text as a top-level `system` instruction and ask for the remainder","D. Retry the identical request; truncation position varies, so a full answer arrives eventually"],a:0,
e:"**Assistant prefill returns `400` on current models** (\"does not support assistant message prefill\"), so the continue-from-partial-turn technique is legacy — recognise it in old code, never recommend it. The remedies are to give the generation enough room (raise `max_tokens`, and stream when the cap is large so the request does not outlive the client timeout) or to restructure the task into sections your code requests and assembles. **Currency note:** this is one of the biggest behavioural breaks between older tutorials and current models.",
w:"B — a `tool_result` must answer a real `tool_use` id and would be rejected; it also mislabels model output as tool output. C — the system prompt is for instructions, and pasting prior output there changes its authority without solving truncation. D — truncation at a fixed cap is not random; the same request truncates at the same length."},

{id:1062,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which pair of response values together confirms that a response was cut short by the cap you configured, rather than by any other limit?",
o:["A. `stop_reason: \"end_turn\"` with a non-null `stop_sequence`","B. `stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"` with `usage.output_tokens` at the configured cap","C. `stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"` with `usage.input_tokens` at the window size","D. A non-null `stop_details` object with `usage.output_tokens` above zero"],a:1,
e:"**`stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"`** names the cause and **`usage.output_tokens`** corroborates it by sitting at your ceiling. Keeping the pair together in telemetry is what lets you distinguish \"our cap is too tight\" from \"the context window filled\" (`model_context_window_exceeded`) — different diagnoses with opposite fixes: raise the cap versus reduce the input. Note the tokens were generated and are billed, so a rising `max_tokens` rate is a cost signal as well as a quality signal.",
w:"A — `end_turn` means a natural finish, and `stop_sequence` is null unless a sequence matched. C — that pair indicates the context window filled, not your cap. D — `stop_details` is populated only on `refusal`."},

{id:1063,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What conversation state does the Messages API retain between two requests from the same client?",
o:["A. The last ten turns, evicted on a rolling basis","B. The system prompt and tool definitions, which need not be resent","C. None — the API is stateless, and the full history must be transmitted on every request","D. Everything, keyed by the API key, until the session times out"],a:2,
e:"The Messages API is **stateless**. Nothing is retained: the model sees exactly the `system`, `tools` and `messages` you transmit on that call and nothing else. Every consequence in this domain follows from it — history must be resent and is re-billed as input each turn, conversation persistence is your application's job, and prompt caching exists precisely because you are resending the same prefix repeatedly. It also confers freedom: because history is a value you own, you can edit, branch or truncate it between calls in ways a server-side session would forbid.",
w:"A — no rolling window of turns is kept server-side. B — `system` and `tools` are part of every request and are re-billed each time. D — there is no session concept and no key-scoped conversation store."},

{id:1064,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A service retries a request after a network read timeout, assuming the retry will return \"the same answer\" if the first call had in fact completed. Downstream consumers occasionally receive two materially different versions of the same generated document. What is the flawed assumption?",
o:["A. Retries are served from a response cache, so difference indicates cache corruption","B. Setting `temperature: 0` would make retries return byte-identical output","C. The response `id` is stable across retries, so deduplication should have caught it","D. Generation is not reproducible, so a retry produces a new response — persist the first result rather than re-deriving it"],a:3,
e:"There is **no request idempotency and no server-side result store**: a retry is a fresh generation, and output is **not fully deterministic**. Correctness therefore depends on your side — write the response to durable storage keyed by your own operation id before acknowledging, and on retry read that record instead of re-generating. **Currency note:** `temperature` (with `top_p` and `top_k`) is deprecated and returns `400` on current models; even where it was supported, `0` never guaranteed identical output. Steer with `output_config.effort` and prompting.",
w:"A — there is no response cache serving retries; each call generates afresh. B — sampling parameters are deprecated and `400` on current models, and `0` was never a determinism guarantee. C — each call returns a new `id`, so dedup on `id` cannot detect a duplicate operation."},

{id:1065,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A support assistant shows per-message cost climbing steadily through long conversations: turn 40 costs many times turn 2, even though the user's messages are the same length. Output tokens are flat. What shape is this, and what is the first lever?",
o:["A. Input grows each turn because the whole history is resent, so cache the stable prefix and bound history length","B. Output pricing rises with conversation depth, so shorten the assistant's replies","C. The model re-tokenises earlier turns less efficiently as they age; periodically reformat them","D. Rate-limit throttling adds a surcharge per retry, so the cost curve is really a retry curve"],a:0,
e:"Statelessness means turn *n* transmits every prior turn, so **input tokens grow with conversation length** and total cost over a session grows roughly with the square of the turn count. The levers, in order: **prompt caching** on the stable prefix (rendered **tools → system → messages**, so cache reads bill far below base input), then bounding history — summarise or drop old turns, and clear stale tool results, which are usually the bulkiest thing in an agent transcript.",
w:"B — output pricing is per token and constant; flat output tokens rule this out. C — tokenisation is deterministic and has no notion of message age. D — throttling causes latency and `429`s, not a per-request surcharge."},

{id:1066,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team deploying a Claude chat service behind a load balancer configures sticky sessions \"so each conversation keeps talking to the same instance and doesn't lose context\". During a rolling deploy, users on drained instances lose their conversations entirely. What is the design error?",
o:["A. Sticky sessions should have been keyed on API key rather than on client IP","B. Conversation state was kept in instance memory; because the API is stateless, history belongs in shared durable storage","C. The load balancer must forward the response `id` so the next instance can resume the conversation","D. Each instance needs its own API key so conversations do not interleave"],a:1,
e:"Stickiness only helps if the instance holds something irreplaceable — which reveals the actual mistake. The Claude API keeps no session, so **your** store is the single source of truth for a conversation; put it in a shared database or cache and any instance can serve any turn, making the service horizontally scalable and deploy-safe. Stickiness then becomes an optimisation at most. Transferable principle: statelessness upstream should push you toward stateless application instances, not toward pinning users to servers.",
w:"A — the stickiness key is irrelevant when the state should not be instance-local at all. C — the response `id` is not a resumption handle; no server-side context exists to resume. D — API keys scope credentials and quota, not conversations."},

{id:1067,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"Which parts of a request must be transmitted again on every call because the API retains none of them?",
o:["A. Only the newest `user` message; earlier turns are retained for the life of the API key","B. The `messages` history only; `system` and `tools` persist once registered","C. The top-level `system`, the `tools` definitions, and the entire `messages` history","D. Nothing beyond `model` and the newest message; everything else is inferred from the previous call"],a:2,
e:"All three: **`system`, `tools` and the full `messages` array** go on every request, and all three are billed as input every time. Tools are the surprise for many teams — there is no registration step, so a large tool surface is a standing per-call cost, plus a fixed tool-use system prompt overhead. This is also why prefix stability matters so much: because you are resending the same bytes repeatedly, a byte-identical prefix can be cached, and any incidental change (a timestamp, an unsorted JSON key order) silently destroys the saving.",
w:"A — no turns are retained; the API key scopes credentials, not conversations. B — `system` and `tools` are not registered anywhere and must accompany each request. D — nothing is inferred from a previous call; there is no previous call from the server's point of view."},

{id:1068,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A user has the same conversation open in two browser tabs. Both tabs hold their own copy of the history, both send a message, and each writes the resulting transcript back. One user's turn silently disappears from the stored conversation. What does this reveal?",
o:["A. The API deduplicates concurrent requests sharing a prefix, discarding one","B. The second request invalidated the first request's cached prefix, dropping its turn","C. Sticky routing was misconfigured, so the two tabs reached different backends","D. With no server-side transcript, concurrent writes to your own store need explicit arbitration"],a:3,
e:"Because the API holds no transcript, **your store is the only transcript**, and two clients each holding a private copy is a classic lost-update race: last writer wins and the loser's turn vanishes. Fix it in your persistence layer — append-only writes rather than whole-document replacement, optimistic concurrency on a version counter, or a single writer per conversation. Statelessness is not just a billing and caching fact; it makes conversation integrity, including its concurrency semantics, entirely your responsibility.",
w:"A — no cross-request deduplication occurs; both calls are generated and billed independently. B — cache invalidation affects cost and latency, never the contents of your stored history. C — routing is irrelevant when the state is shared; the conflict is at the write, not the read."},

{id:1069,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A mis-worded user prompt triggers `stop_reason: \"refusal\"`. The team worries that the refusal will now sit in the history and colour the rest of the session. What does the API's design permit here?",
o:["A. Drop the offending user turn and the refusal from `messages` before the next request, then re-ask","B. Nothing — history is immutable once a turn has been generated against it","C. Send a `system` message ordering the model to disregard the previous exchange","D. Start a new conversation, since the refusal is bound to the session on Anthropic's side"],a:0,
e:"Because the API is **stateless**, the history is just an array you own — you can remove the failed exchange and re-ask cleanly, and the model has no memory of the discarded turn. This same property enables branching (two divergent futures from one shared prefix) and re-generation from an earlier point. Two practical caveats: editing near the front of the prefix costs you the prompt cache from the edit onward, and some models bind replayed `thinking` blocks such that editing earlier turns invalidates them — so prefer trimming the tail over rewriting the middle.",
w:"B — nothing about the history is immutable; the server retains no copy to contradict you. C — an instruction to ignore visible context is advisory and weaker than simply not sending it. D — no session exists to be tainted; a brand-new conversation is unnecessary."},

{id:1070,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"An autonomous research agent appends every tool result to its history and never removes anything. It works well on short tasks. On long runs it eventually fails, and the team's plan is to move to the largest-context model available. What should they be told?",
o:["A. Large-context models remove the constraint, so the plan is sound as it stands","B. Unbounded history growth exhausts any finite window; a bounded-context strategy is required, and a bigger window only postpones the failure","C. Tool results are exempt from the context window, so the failure must have another cause","D. Setting a higher `max_tokens` will let the agent finish before the window is reached"],a:1,
e:"Monotonic growth against a finite window fails at some run length, whatever the window is — a larger model buys headroom, not a fix. The strategy has to bound context: clear stale tool results, summarise or compact completed phases, keep results compact at the tool boundary (a bulky result is re-billed on every later iteration), and handle **`stop_reason: \"model_context_window_exceeded\"`** as a real state rather than a crash. Remember the window bounds request **plus** generated output including thinking tokens, so a near-full prompt cannot answer at all.",
w:"A — it postpones the failure rather than removing it, and costs more per call meanwhile. C — tool results are ordinary input content and consume the window like anything else. D — `max_tokens` caps output; it cannot create window space and a higher cap consumes more of it."},

{id:1071,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What does setting `output_config.format` to `{\"type\":\"json_schema\",\"schema\":{...}}` do?",
o:["A. Adds a post-generation validation step that retries automatically on a schema mismatch","B. Instructs the model in the system prompt to try to follow the schema","C. Constrains decoding so the response is guaranteed to be schema-valid JSON","D. Converts the response into a `tool_use` block whose `input` matches the schema"],a:2,
e:"`output_config.format` uses **constrained decoding**: the schema is compiled into a grammar and generation is restricted to tokens that keep the output valid, so schema-conformant JSON is a guarantee rather than an instruction. **No beta header is required.** Two operational notes: the first request with a new schema pays a one-time grammar-compilation latency, after which compiled grammars are cached for 24 hours from last use; and the guarantee covers a *completed* response — a `max_tokens` truncation or a `refusal` can still leave you without valid output, so keep checking `stop_reason`.",
w:"A — the constraint is applied during generation; there is no retry loop. B — prompt instructions are advisory, which is exactly what this feature replaces. D — the response remains a normal message with `text` content; tool blocks are a separate mechanism."},

{id:1072,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A codebase extracts structured data by defining a dummy `record_result` tool whose `input_schema` is the desired shape, forcing `tool_choice` to it, and reading `tool_use.input`. A new engineer asks whether this is still the right pattern. What should they be told?",
o:["A. Keep it — it is the only mechanism that guarantees schema conformance","B. Keep it but drop the forced `tool_choice`, since forcing is what makes it fragile","C. Replace it with a system-prompt instruction plus client-side validation and retries","D. Replace it with `output_config.format`, or `strict: true` on a real tool — both supersede the workaround"],a:3,
e:"The dummy-tool trick existed because there was no native mechanism; there is now. **`output_config.format`** constrains the response itself, and **`strict: true`** on a genuine tool definition guarantees its `input` validates. Both use constrained decoding and neither needs a beta header. The workaround also inherits the constraints of forced tool use: `tool_choice` `any`/`tool` errors under manual extended thinking and returns `400` on some models, whereas `auto` and `none` always work. Choose by what you are constraining — the model's answer, or its call arguments.",
w:"A — native structured outputs provide the stronger guarantee; the workaround no longer holds that position. B — removing the force makes it unreliable rather than fragile, and still leaves a fake tool in the surface. C — instruction plus retry is weaker and more expensive than constrained decoding."},

{id:1073,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team defines a structured-output schema including `\"minimum\": 0`, `\"maxLength\": 50` and `\"additionalProperties\": true`. Responses come back as valid JSON but occasionally carry a negative number and an over-long string. What is going on?",
o:["A. Numeric and string constraints are not enforced by constrained decoding, and `additionalProperties` must be `false` — enforce those bounds in your own validation layer","B. Those keywords are enforced only when `strict: true` is also set on a tool","C. The schema silently fell back to unconstrained generation because it was too large to compile","D. Constraints apply only to the first object in a response; nested objects are unconstrained"],a:0,
e:"Structured outputs support a defined subset of JSON Schema: basic types, `enum`, `const`, `anyOf`, `allOf`, `$ref`/`$defs`, the standard string formats — and require **`additionalProperties: false`** on objects. **Numerical constraints (`minimum`, `maximum`, `multipleOf`), string-length constraints and recursive schemas are not supported.** The Python and TypeScript SDKs strip unsupported keywords before sending and validate them client-side, which is why behaviour differs between SDK and raw-HTTP paths. The principle: constrained decoding guarantees *shape*, not *business rules* — validate value ranges yourself.",
w:"B — `strict: true` governs tool inputs and enforces the same supported subset; it does not add numeric bounds. C — an unsupported schema is rejected or stripped, not silently downgraded to free generation. D — the constraint applies to the whole document, nesting included."},

{id:1074,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A document assistant must both cite its sources and return a strict JSON envelope for a downstream service. The team enables `citations` on their `document` blocks and sets `output_config.format`. Every request returns `400`. What is the resolution?",
o:["A. Move the citations flag from the document blocks to `output_config` so both features share a config","B. Split the work: one call with citations for grounding, a second call that structures the result — the two features cannot be combined on one request","C. Set `strict: true` on a tool instead; that combination is permitted with citations","D. Enable citations on only some document blocks so the incompatibility is not triggered"],a:1,
e:"**Structured output via `output_config.format` is incompatible with citations and returns `400`.** The clean resolution is decomposition — a grounded, citation-bearing call, then a second, cheap call (or plain parsing code) that reshapes the result into the envelope. That also reflects a broader habit worth forming: when two API features conflict, split the pipeline at the conflict rather than searching for a flag that reconciles them. Note also that citations must be enabled on **all** document blocks or none.",
w:"A — `citations` is a per-document-block setting; there is no `output_config` equivalent, and relocating it would not remove the conflict. C — the conflict is with structured output generally; substituting the tool-side feature does not license citations. D — partial enabling is itself invalid, and the incompatibility is not per-block."},

{id:1075,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"E",
q:"What is the difference between `output_config.format` and `strict: true` on a tool definition?",
o:["A. They are aliases; `strict: true` is the older spelling of the same feature","B. `output_config.format` validates after generation; `strict: true` validates during it","C. `output_config.format` constrains what Claude says; `strict: true` constrains how Claude calls your tools","D. `output_config.format` applies to streamed responses; `strict: true` applies to non-streamed ones"],a:2,
e:"They solve different problems and compose: **`output_config.format` constrains the model's response content** to your JSON Schema, while **`strict: true` on a tool guarantees that tool's `input` validates exactly** against its `input_schema`. Choose by what you need guaranteed — the answer you parse, or the arguments you execute. Both use constrained decoding, both require `additionalProperties: false` plus `required`, and neither needs a beta header. `strict: true` is a top-level field on the tool definition, not something you put inside `tool_choice`.",
w:"A — they are distinct features that can be used together in one request. B — both act during generation via constrained decoding. D — neither is tied to streaming mode; both work streamed or buffered."},

{id:1076,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"After a model upgrade, a working extraction service starts returning `400` on every call. The only Claude-specific code is a helper that appends `{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"{\"}` as the final message so replies begin with a JSON object. What changed, and what replaces it?",
o:["A. The brace must now be escaped as `\\u007b` in assistant content","B. Assistant messages may no longer be the last element; move it before the user turn","C. `content` must be an array of blocks in that position; wrap the brace in a `text` block","D. Assistant prefill is rejected on current models — use `output_config.format` to guarantee JSON instead"],a:3,
e:"The `{`-prefill trick relied on a trailing `assistant` turn being continued, and **assistant message prefill now returns `400` on current models** (\"does not support assistant message prefill\"). It is legacy: know it well enough to recognise it in old code and never recommend it. The replacement is stronger anyway — **`output_config.format`** with a `json_schema` guarantees schema-valid JSON by constrained decoding, so you get conformance rather than merely a plausible opening character, and you stop string-surgery on the response.",
w:"A — the failure is the prefill itself, not the character or its encoding. B — an assistant turn is perfectly legal in the middle of a history; it is the trailing prefill position that is rejected. C — the block form is valid syntax but changes nothing; the prefill is still rejected."},

{id:1077,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A pipeline pre-checks prompt size with `tiktoken` before dispatching to Claude, rejecting anything over the window. Requests still overflow in production, most often on source-code payloads. What is the correct pre-check?",
o:["A. `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens` with the same `model` you will use for inference","B. `tiktoken` with a 20% safety margin, which is the documented correction factor","C. A character count divided by four, which is more reliable than any tokenizer library","D. A trial `POST /v1/messages` with `max_tokens: 1`, reading `usage.input_tokens`"],a:0,
e:"Tokenizers are **model-specific**, and `tiktoken` is another vendor's — it undercounts Claude tokens on ordinary prose and much more on code and non-English text. Use **`/v1/messages/count_tokens`**, passing the same `model` you will send the real request to, and read `input_tokens`. It accepts the same structured input as a Messages request, including `system`, `tools`, images and PDFs, so what you measure is what you will send. Treat the figure as an **estimate** and keep headroom.",
w:"B — there is no published correction factor, and the error varies with content type. C — characters-per-token varies by tokenizer and content; roughly 4 on older models and nearer 2.5 on newer ones. D — a real generation call costs money and still measures only one request; the free counting endpoint exists for this."},

{id:1078,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"H",
q:"A team sizes its chunking strategy using token counts measured months ago against an older model. After moving to a newer model — with the same nominal context window — requests that used to fit now return `model_context_window_exceeded`, and per-request cost is noticeably higher. What happened?",
o:["A. The context window is smaller in practice on newer models despite the same nominal figure","B. The newer tokenizer produces roughly 30% more tokens for the same text; counts must be re-measured per model","C. Counts drift because the counting endpoint returns an estimate that degrades over time","D. Thinking tokens are now charged as input, inflating both counts and cost"],a:1,
e:"Newer models use a **newer tokenizer that yields roughly 30% more tokens for identical text** — about 2.5 characters per token versus about 4 previously. Billing and window occupancy both follow the tokenizer, so cached counts from an older model understate everything on the newer one. The discipline: re-baseline with `count_tokens` against the exact `model` you plan to use, on your own representative content, and never migrate a chunk-size constant across a tokenizer boundary. Counting the same request under both models quantifies the delta directly.",
w:"A — the nominal window is the real window; what changed is how much text fits inside it. C — estimates do not degrade over time; they are computed per request against the model you name. D — thinking tokens are counted as output when generated, and as input only when replayed in prior turns; that is not the effect here."},

{id:1079,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"R",f:"M",
q:"What does a successful `POST /v1/messages/count_tokens` response contain?",
o:["A. `input_tokens` and `estimated_output_tokens`","B. A `usage` object identical in shape to a Messages response","C. A single `input_tokens` field covering the messages, the system prompt and the tools","D. `token_count` plus a `cost_estimate` in the account currency"],a:2,
e:"The response is a single field: **`input_tokens`**, the total across the provided `messages`, `system` prompt and `tools`. It cannot report output — output does not exist until generation happens — which is why the endpoint needs no `max_tokens`. It is **tokenizer-specific to the `model` you pass** and is documented as an **estimate**; actual billed input may differ slightly, and Anthropic notes that counts may include system-added tokens you are not billed for. Use it to fit prompts, route between models and pre-check spend, not to reconcile invoices.",
w:"A — no output estimate is returned; output length is unknowable in advance. B — the shape is a dedicated one-field object, not a `usage` object. D — no monetary figure is returned; converting tokens to cost is yours to do."},

{id:1080,d:"D1",s:"Claude API Mechanics",t:"S",f:"M",
q:"A team wants to call `count_tokens` before every Messages request to enforce a per-tenant prompt-size policy. An architect objects that this will \"double our API load and eat our rate limits\". What is the accurate assessment?",
o:["A. The objection holds: counting requests consume the same requests-per-minute allowance as generation","B. The objection holds on cost grounds: counting is billed at input rates for the tokens counted","C. Counting should be done once per conversation, since the endpoint is stateful and caches the prefix","D. Counting is free and has its own rate limit independent of message creation — but the count is an estimate, so leave headroom"],a:3,
e:"Token counting is **free** and carries **its own requests-per-minute limit, independent of message creation** — usage of one does not count against the other. So a pre-flight count is cheap and does not compete with generation capacity; the real constraints are the extra round-trip latency and the counting endpoint's own RPM ceiling at high volume. Because the figure is an **estimate**, enforce policy with a margin rather than at the exact window boundary. Note also that counting does not exercise prompt caching, even if you include `cache_control` blocks.",
w:"A — the two endpoints have separate, independent rate limits. B — the endpoint is free to use; no input charge applies. C — the endpoint is stateless and performs no caching; each call counts what you send it."}
);
